<Sequential id="uk_12">

<Vertical id="uk_12_s1" title="Months later">
<Markdown id="uk_12_s1_text">
Months later, Zofia flattens the letter on the kitchen table. There is a tea ring across the signature. She checks that she can still read the name.

Ewa puts a clean mug on a saucer beside it. Zofia moves the letter away from both.

“The animals?” Ewa asks.

Zofia turns the page. Len's application number. Hale's. Nothing from the private publisher about the words “all service was voluntary”. She checks the back.

The Records (Correction) Act—the law they campaigned for—covers designated UK-government historical accounts. It cannot make that publisher answer. The Workshop's animal-service claim is still with its named voluntary reviewer.

But the Historical Accounts Office must now search and answer applications from living representatives for Len and Hale, giving evidence and reasons, with independent review and appeal. Zofia puts a finger beside each number.

It took months, a new session and a reintroduced Bill. Mara, a Labour government backbencher, still had to negotiate government support and time. The first reading before the slam had introduced a Bill, not made a law.

Zofia can put those months between two paragraphs. Mara couldn't put them between two knocks.

Under the letter, Ewa has left a shopping list. Its last item shows through the thin paper: bread.
</Markdown>
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="uk_12_s2" title="Versions">
<Markdown id="uk_12_versions_scene">
*During those months, in Mara's campaign meeting room, Morwenna lays two versions of the Bill across the table.*
</Markdown>
<Chat id="uk_12_versions_1" src="silence.chatpeg" clip="[1, 9]"/>
<Markdown id="uk_12_versions_transition" when="@uk_12_versions_1.value &gt;= 9">
*Mara goes back to her party's organisers without accepting their offer. Committee stage follows, then report and third reading in the Commons. The search duty survives. In the Lords, written reasons and an appeal are added; the argument over staff time starts again.*
</Markdown>
<Chat id="uk_12_versions_2" src="silence.chatpeg" clip="[10, 16]" when="@uk_12_versions_1.value &gt;= 9"/>
<Markdown id="uk_12_versions_assent" when="@uk_12_versions_2.value &gt;= 16">
*Both Houses eventually agree the same text. Royal Assent makes it an Act.*
</Markdown>
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="uk_12_s3" title="The room without a reply">
<Markdown id="uk_12_s3_text">
They go to the Association room on a wet afternoon. Ollie's umbrella folds inside out before he gets it through the entrance. He carries it downstairs like something he has arrested.

The room smells of wet coats and a radiator warming dust. Len's tablet is where it was. So is the scuffed chair Zofia used to pull round so she could face him without looking as though she were talking to the wall.

She leaves the chair facing the table.

The Association has given them permission to use the room. The answer came from somewhere else: the Historical Accounts Office, whose unit-account custodian has signed it. The covering sheet identifies the application, lists the evidence searched and gives the decision date. Ollie checks that the attachment is the published version. He has brought the earlier holding reply too. Its promise to consider future policy fits on half a page.

The new answer takes several pages. One corner has caught in the staple. Zofia eases it free with her nail rather than tearing it.

She has heard Ollie talk all the way here: about the rain, a missed bus, somebody who has apparently become somebody else's boyfriend. Now he holds the failed umbrella between his shoes and waits for her to begin.
</Markdown>
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="uk_12_s4" title="The purpose annotation">
<Markdown id="uk_12_len_answer_scene">
